<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 9 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">Empires of Africa</h1><p class="path-tagline">Gold, faith and trade in the great states of Nubia, Ethiopia and West Africa.</p><p class="path-intro">Africa's empires were built on control of gold, trade routes and the loyalty of diverse peoples. Kush ruled Egypt as its Twenty-Fifth Dynasty; Aksum struck its own gold coins and traded with Rome and India; and in West Africa a succession of states, Ghana, Mali and Songhai, grew rich on the trans-Saharan exchange of gold and salt. This path follows that story from the Nile to the forest kingdom of Asante, whose Golden Stool embodied the soul of the nation.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Timekeeper</a> · <a href="/start">the Navigator</a></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/kushite-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/meroe.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Profile · 1070 BCE – 350 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Kushite Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">From Napata, Piye and his successors united Nubia and Egypt as the Twenty-Fifth Dynasty, before Meroë became a great centre of iron.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/aksumite-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ezana.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Profile · 100 CE – 940 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Aksumite Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Controlling the Red Sea port of Adulis, Aksum issued its own gold coins and conquered Kush around 350 CE.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/ghana-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/walata.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Profile · 300 CE – 1240 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Ghana Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">The Soninke 'land of gold' taxed both salt moving south and gold moving north, reserving every gold nugget for the king.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/sundiata-keita"><span class="path-step-ph">4</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Encyclopedia · Person · d. c. 1255</span><span class="path-step-name">Sundiata Keita</span><span class="path-step-why">The founder of Mali, whose victory at Kirina united the Manding chiefdoms and whose epic is still preserved by griots.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/mali-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/djenne.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Profile · 1235 CE – 1600 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Mali Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Mali controlled the goldfields of Bambuk and Bure and made Timbuktu and Djenné centres of trade and Islamic learning.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musas-pilgrimage"><span class="path-step-ph">6</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1324</span><span class="path-step-name">Mansa Musa's pilgrimage</span><span class="path-step-why">Mansa Musa's lavish spending of gold in Cairo in 1324 put Mali on the map, quite literally in the Catalan Atlas.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/songhai-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gao.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Profile · 1430 CE – 1600 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Songhai Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">The largest empire in West African history, built by Sunni Ali and Askia Muhammad on the cities of Gao, Timbuktu and Djenné.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tondibi"><span class="path-step-ph">8</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1591</span><span class="path-step-name">Battle of Tondibi</span><span class="path-step-why">In 1591 a small Moroccan force armed with arquebuses routed a much larger Songhai army in a single engagement.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/ashanti-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/golden-stool.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Profile · 1670 CE – 1902 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Ashanti Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Osei Tutu I and the priest Okomfo Anokye united the Akan chiefdoms around the Golden Stool and defeated Denkyira at Feyiase in 1701.</span></span></a></li></ol>
